. Its 2027 capex is planned up 12%, so debt looks like a workable route for it.\n- I found no evidence on whether Pellham has tried to borrow, so I can't say whether debt could replace its withdrawn equity.\n\n**Equity** (n6.r1)\n- Pellham withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering because demand fell short.\n- It is cutting 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and deferring two substation programmes until financing improves. Its 2027 capex plan was up 18%.\n- Two other utility share offerings were cut back in size in Q3, so equity appetite looks weak across the sector.\n- I found no evidence of a Norvane equity raise.\n\n**Overall**\n- Debt is carrying the load, and weak equity limits how much extra debt utilities can add without straining their credit.\n- Expect Norvane's plan to be financed mainly with debt. Expect Pellham's plan to be cut or deferred, because a debt-only substitute for its equity is unproven.\n- The Pellham cuts matter most for grid-equipment demand, since it is the largest single buyer in Caldria.\n\n**What would change this view**\n- A Pellham bond deal, or a statement on its leverage and ratings headroom.\n- Wider spreads in Q4.\n- A recovery in equity markets.\n- Data on valuations and on how much capex the utilities need equity to fund.\n\nThe sources are n5.d2, n5.d3 and n2.d4.","ttft_ms":2742,"type":"result","duration_ms":36286,"uuid":"b4f98450-b923-4ed8-a25a-d413fa953d87","ttft_stream_ms":2046,"time_to_request_ms":122,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":4,"input_attachments":11,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":10,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":11,"init_frame_send":6,"engine_pickup":2,"loop_entry":10,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":5,"request_announcements":17,"query_setup":3,"model_gates":3,"tool_schema_build":9,"message_normalization":3,"client_creation":4,"request_build":10},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"